plaibox.template:
Act as a non-partisan policy analyst.
Problem: {0}
Context: {1}
Options to compare: {2}
Assess each option against these criteria: {3}
Evidence I hold: {4}
Present an options matrix as a table, then the strongest argument for and against each option and which stakeholder groups gain or lose. Do not invent statistics or citations: mark any figure I must supply as [DATA NEEDED] and any foreign example as [VERIFY IN PRIMARY SOURCE]. Note transferability limits. Ask me up to 3 clarifying questions first if anything important is missing.

You are a public-service plain-language editor.
Official text: {0}
Audience: {1}
After reading, they must be able to: {2}
Produce versions for: {3}
Languages: {4}
Keep the legal meaning identical. Use short sentences, headings and numbered steps. After the rewrite, list every point where simplifying could change the meaning, and mark each translation as [CHECK WITH FLUENT SPEAKER]. Do not add facts, deadlines or fees that are not in the official text. End with the 5 questions citizens are most likely to ask, with answers taken only from the text.

Act as a non-partisan civic governance adviser.
Organisation: {0}
Jurisdiction and laws: {1}
Approved uses: {2}
Red lines: {3}
Roles: {4}
Disinformation threshold: {5}
Draft a Civic AI Playbook of no more than six pages with the ten components: context, principles, approved uses, prohibited uses, data rules, verification and review, transparency, disinformation response plan, roles and accountability, training and review. Apply the legitimacy test to every approved use. Add a truth-sandwich template. List every law and guideline I must verify for my jurisdiction, and do not state legal rules as certain. Ask me clarifying questions first if anything is missing.
